# Line endings are a correctness property here, not a style preference.
#
# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true`, and with no attributes file every
# checkout on Windows rewrites LF to CRLF. Shell survives that almost
# everywhere, which is what makes it expensive: bash does not treat CR as
# whitespace, so it lands inside the last token on a line. `BOTH="a b"` assigns
# `a b<CR>`, a heredoc writes the CR into the file it generates, and the value
# travels until something compares it. That is how `verify-gh-account-guard`
# came to test `chpark-ML<CR>` against `chpark-ML` and report eight failures in
# a hook that was working correctly.
#
# The blobs in this repository are already LF; only the checkout was wrong. So
# `eol=lf` changes no content — it stops the checkout from introducing a
# difference that exists on one platform and nowhere else.
#
# After adding this file an existing clone still holds the old bytes. Refresh
# it once:  git add --renormalize . && git checkout-index -a -f
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries must never be touched by any of the above.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.pdf binary
*.ico binary
